oing to be two weeks  four weeks and here we are a year later  and  just amazing how dedicated everybody was  [Music]  so here we find feife at the end of the  movie  she's gone to the moon and back and  gone through an incredible healing  learning to love somebody new  and they have this wonderful bond  together  but this scene is really about  a time for the audience to reflect  i feel like this scene is really the  moment  um where we see fife connect  with her mother in the form of this  beautiful white bird and  that they are able to have this moment  of peace together  where the mother knows that fife is  healed  and that she can move on as well and  it's  so beautiful the acting feels so natural  it was just the way that  faith phase kneels down on  to that stone kids are so flexible she  can have both of her  knees kind of stretched out flat jenny  reminded me  of your son that he would come to  work and you'd see him just fold up like  that and  it was like so i storyboarded this  thinking of him and the way he  kneeled knelt down and  it's really just a testament to  the animation team that put that kind of  truth in this and that scarf represents  her mom  too it's a drawing actually i did of  chong  that we placed on that scarf  and in the beginning you watch  those um that silk design  turn into hand-drawn animation it was  very important for me that  changa be represented  in the most referential way because we  were going to be  borrowing changa from the chinese  culture and we were basically going to  turn her  upside down on her head and we had to  bring her back to that place of dignity  and when i look at  this moment here where fei-fei looks at  changha  on that scarf i'm so happy that  what we present is a change that  has healed is deeper  is warmer is it it's all in that  expression there's a dignity and there's  a grace  to her and that's what right after it  feife  holds that scarf and treasures the  memory that she's had  of changa and her 